{
  "difficulty": "intermediate",
  "score": 5,
  "estimated_minutes": 45,
  "factors": {
    "transaction_background": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "The reviewer must understand HRCI’s territorial trademark license, the licensees’ sublicensed properties, and the different objectives of the arbitration motion and motions to dismiss counterclaims, but need not reconstruct the equity holders’ corporate history."
    },
    "domain_knowledge": {
      "level": "low",
      "note": "The trademark-license and FAA background is explained sufficiently in the opinion, and the disputes over quality, exclusivity, websites, and promotional cooperation require no technical industry knowledge."
    },
    "defect_spread": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "The Rehab and sublicensing constructions require reading §§ 1(l), 2(a), 4(a), 5(a), 5(d), and 10 together, while territorial exclusivity also requires § 3(a)’s reservation of rights."
    },
    "court_reasoning": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "The court generally states the competing readings clearly, but the reviewer must distinguish its arbitration-scope constructions from merits determinations and its findings of ambiguity from definitive interpretations of § 8."
    },
    "issue_distinctness": {
      "level": "high",
      "note": "The same Rehab passage supports distinct constructions of Licensed Services, promotional authorization, quality standards, arbitration coverage, and the arbitration exclusion, making these issues harder to distinguish than the website disputes."
    }
  },
  "hardest_defect": "d06 — Understanding the § 5(d) exclusion requires separating HRCI’s infringement and termination labels from the quality-control facts, then relating the exclusion to the affirmative arbitration obligation and the scope of authorized uses. The reviewer must also preserve the distinction between the unaltered-mark uses referred to arbitration and other claims not resolved on this motion.",
  "reason": "Assign this to a reviewer comfortable mapping related contract provisions and separating arbitrability from the underlying merits; trademark specialization is unnecessary. The main challenge is distinguishing the layered constructions supporting arbitration, rather than understanding the hotel business or corporate ownership structure. The exclusivity dispute requires checking the grant against reserved rights, whereas the website and advertising disputes are mostly explicit competing readings of ordinary language. A focused reread should leave the reviewer confident, provided they record that several § 8 issues remain ambiguous and that the best-efforts challenge was deferred at the pleading stage."
}
